<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julio C. Tello</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1880">1880</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Julio César Tello</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julio C. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Tello</a> was a Peruvian archaeologist, often called the father of Peruvian archaeology, who argued that Andean civilisation developed locally. He studied Chavín de Huántar, and in 1925 discovered the Paracas cemeteries on the south coast, excavating the necropolis of Wari Kayan with hundreds of mummy bundles wrapped in embroidered textiles. He founded museums of archaeology in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julio-c-tello.jpg" alt="Julio C. Tello" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Julio_C._Tello_Harvard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Tello</a> was born on 11 April 1880 in an Andean village of Huarochirí Province to a family that spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a>, and is regarded as the first indigenous archaeologist in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. He persuaded the Peruvian government to fund his education and completed a bachelor's degree in medicine at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-university-of-san-marcos">National University of San Marcos</a> in 1909. As a student he studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trepanning">trepanation</a> among the people of Huarochirí and gathered a large collection of skulls, which became the basis of a university collection.</p><p>With a scholarship from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard University</a> he learned English and earned a master's degree in anthropology in 1911. He then studied archaeology in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and in 1912, at the Congress of Americanists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, he met Olive Mabel Cheesman, whom he married that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1919, working at Chavín de Huantar, he discovered the stele now called the Tello Obelisk. His work and that of others showed the site had been the centre of a complex culture lasting several hundred years. On the Paracas Peninsula, which he first visited on 26 July 1925, his team uncovered on 25 October 1927 the first of 429 mummy bundles, bodies seated in baskets and wrapped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth embroidered in wool. He was the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> to excavate by a scientific method that preserved stratigraphy. His team collected 394 textiles, won Rockefeller Foundation funds to preserve them, and had more than 180 on display by 1938.</p><p>In 1936 he helped found the Institute for Andean Research with Alfred Kroeber, Samuel Lothrop and Wendell C. Bennett, and on 3 January 1939 he became the first director of the new national museum of anthropology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Tello</a> died on 3 June 1947. Against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-uhle">Max Uhle</a>, who held that Andean culture came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, he argued that it emerged and developed in place, and he saw the highlands as a centre of ancient culture. Because he published in little-known journals and newspapers, his ideas long reached few readers outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>; Richard L. Burger's The Life and Writings of Julio C. Tello (2009) made them more widely available. His student Rebeca Carrión Cachot succeeded him as director, and a museum on the Paracas Peninsula bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Julio C. Tello” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Julio C. Tello</b>, who worked at the site from 1919, saw Chavín as the ‘mother culture’ of Andean civilisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chavin">Chavín · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It is best known from its cemeteries: the necropolis of Wari Kayan, excavated by <b>Julio C. Tello</b> in 1927, held more than 400 mummy bundles wrapped in brilliantly coloured embroidered mantles of cotton and camelid wool.</blockquote><a href="/state/paracas">Paracas</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Huarochirí Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-11.85,-76.38&amp;year=1923" aria-label="Huarochirí Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Julio C. Tello"><image href="/maps/globes/g27000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6129 6006h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6129 6006h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-11.85,-76.38&amp;year=1923">What was here in 1923? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
